pack=json.loads("{\"as_of\":\"2026-10-03\",\"status\":\"prepared_not_published\",\"static_exact_replacements\":[{\"path\":\"programme.html\",\"old\":\"Our clinical partner handles assessment, prescribing and clinical monitoring; a registered pharmacy handles dispensing.\",\"new\":\"The proposed clinical partner service is not open for booking. When available, an appropriately regulated provider would handle assessment, prescribing and clinical monitoring, with a registered pharmacy responsible for dispensing.\",\"reason\":\"Current-service claim conflicts with confirmed closed partner pathway.\",\"source\":\"User-confirmed operating status; /partner-with-us\"},{\"path\":\"decision-centre-methodology.html\",\"old\":\"The national Shift Programme generally requires\",\"new\":\"The NHS Digital Weight Management Programme in England generally requires\",\"reason\":\"Incorrect SHIFT branding and missing geography.\",\"source\":\"https://www.england.nhs.uk/digital-weight-management/\"},{\"path\":\"decision-centre-methodology.html\",\"old\":\"The tool treats this as <strong>current rollout context</strong>, not a permanent national eligibility rule.\",\"new\":\"This describes the initial cohort, not a complete current eligibility check. Later phases and local implementation can widen access; check your ICB’s current route.\",\"reason\":\"Initial cohort presented as complete current rollout.\",\"source\":\"https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/interim-commissioning-guidance-nice-ta1026-tirzepatide/\"},{\"path\":\"treatments/emerging.html\",\"old\":\"Amgen's Phase 3 MARITIME Shift Programme\",\"new\":\"Amgen's Phase 3 MARITIME research programme\",\"reason\":\"Incorrect SHIFT branding of external trial programme.\",\"source\":\"https://www.amgen.com/newsroom/press-releases/2025/06/results-from-amgens-phase-2-obesity-study-of-monthly-maritide-presented-at-the-american-diabetes-association-85th-scientific-sessions\"},{\"path\":\"mens-mental-health.html\",\"old\":\"If you need urgent mental-health help but it is not an emergency, use NHS 111 online or call 111 and select the mental-health option.\",\"new\":\"In England, for urgent mental-health help without immediate danger, use NHS 111 online or call 111 and select the mental-health option. Elsewhere in the UK, use the <a href=\\\"/mental-health/urgent-help\\\">urgent support guide for your nation</a>.\",\"reason\":\"England-specific service presented as UK-wide.\",\"source\":\"https://www.nhs.uk/nhs-services/mental-health-services/where-to-get-urgent-help-for-mental-health/\"},{\"path\":\"mens-mental-health.html\",\"old\":\"What this section will grow into\",\"new\":\"Explore the full Good to Talk library\",\"reason\":\"Existing content described as unreleased.\",\"source\":\"102 captured live mental-health pages\"},{\"path\":\"mens-mental-health.html\",\"old\":\"This first release builds the understanding layer. The next Good to Talk releases will go much further into life's pressure points, practical self-help, getting professional help, helping a mate, urgent support and a large question-led resource library.\",\"new\":\"Explore guidance on life’s pressure points, practical self-help, getting professional help, supporting someone else and finding urgent support. Choose the question closest to what is happening for you.\",\"reason\":\"Remove obsolete future-release promise.\",\"source\":\"102 captured live mental-health pages\"},{\"path\":\"waiting-list-journey.html\",\"old\":\"Be first to hear when stock returns.\",\"new\":\"Hear when treatment becomes available.\",\"reason\":\"No verified previous stock or queue priority.\",\"source\":\"Current no-orders operating status\"},{\"path\":\"privacy-centre.html\",\"old\":\"Optional analytics and marketing stay off unless your cookie choice allows them.\",\"new\":\"Optional analytics stay off unless you consent. Advertising storage and marketing cookies remain off.\",\"reason\":\"Align with current cookie policy; do not imply marketing consent control exists.\",\"source\":\"/cookies\"},{\"path\":\"treatment-centre.html\",\"old\":\"Compare the options Google is already finding\",\"new\":\"Compare common treatment questions\",\"reason\":\"Reader-facing copy should not describe SEO strategy.\",\"source\":\"Editorial correction\"},{\"path\":\"downloads/maintenance-plan-template.html\",\"old\":\"What I will do after regain of 2–3 kg\",\"new\":\"What change would prompt me to review my plan, and with whom\",\"reason\":\"Avoid universal regain threshold as clinical rule.\",\"source\":\"Individual maintenance planning; no validated universal threshold claimed\"},{\"path\":\"start-here.html\",\"old\":\"The strength does not climb; customers select the required pack size.\",\"new\":\"Follow the prescribed strength and meal-linked instructions. A pharmacist or prescriber checks suitability and supply.\",\"reason\":\"Avoid retail choice implying clinical approval.\",\"source\":\"https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/2592/smpc\"}],\"dynamic_exact_replacements\":[{\"path\":\"/shift-health/blood-pressure-monitor\",\"old\":\"Log three properly taken morning readings, then use the appropriate health door if the pattern is high.\",\"new\":\"If home monitoring has been advised, follow the agreed schedule and arrange for the complete record to be reviewed. For diagnostic monitoring, NICE describes two seated readings at least one minute apart, morning and evening, for at least four days and ideally seven; exclude day one when calculating the average.\",\"source\":\"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng136/chapter/recommendations\",\"reason\":\"Three morning readings do not implement cited diagnostic schedule.\"},{\"path\":\"/shift-health/blood-pressure-monitor\",\"old\":\"Record three morning readings\",\"new\":\"Follow the agreed repeat-reading schedule\",\"source\":\"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng136/chapter/recommendations\",\"reason\":\"Remove contradictory short schedule.\"},{\"path\":\"/shift-health/erectile-dysfunction\",\"old\":\"Chest pain during sex, an erection lasting four hours, penile injury, severe pain or sudden neurological symptoms need urgent medical help.\",\"new\":\"For an erection lasting more than 3 to 4 hours, call 999 or go to A&E. If you have sickle cell disease and a painful erection lasts more than one hour, get emergency help. Chest pain or sudden stroke-like symptoms also need emergency help; penile injury or severe pain needs urgent assessment.\",\"source\":\"https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/priapism-painful-erections/\",\"reason\":\"Include earlier sickle-cell emergency threshold and explicit emergency route.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/eating-out-weight-management-men\",\"old\":\"The takeaway page owns cuisine-by-cuisine decisions; the BBQ page owns grills, sides and drinks. They support this owner rather than competing with the broader eating-out intent.\",\"new\":\"For more meal ideas, explore the takeaway and BBQ guides. Choose the advice that fits the meal you are planning.\",\"source\":\"Editorial correction\",\"reason\":\"Internal content strategy leaked into reader copy.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/protein-for-men-weight-management\",\"old\":\"The tool provides selectable planning levels. It must name the multiplier, show that the result is an estimate and refuse to call it a treatment target.\",\"new\":\"The calculator shows the planning multiplier and an estimated result. It is not an individual treatment target.\",\"source\":\"Existing calculator methodology\",\"reason\":\"Internal implementation instruction exposed.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/waist-size-men\",\"old\":\"The calculator must say that beside the number.\",\"new\":\"Read the limitations beside the result before using it to plan a next step.\",\"source\":\"Editorial correction\",\"reason\":\"Internal implementation instruction exposed.\"}],\"additional_required_work\":[\"Update long-article titles/descriptions everywhere they appear, including Explore Knowledge and news cards\",\"Fix Health MOT questionnaire promises and links across Decision Centre, Finder, Tools, Treatment Centre and lifestyle routes; confirm actual accessible legacy questionnaire before choosing destination\",\"Deduplicate repeated introductions in article/short-guide templates without losing distinct summary metadata\",\"Add drug-specific direct product-information links and urgent safety context to Mounjaro/Wegovy/Foundayo hubs\",\"Recheck RO7204239 news against later Chugai discontinuation announcement; registry alone may lag\",\"Reconcile newsroom total 168 versus 167 captured news routes; no captured medicine-news link was missing\",\"Verify remaining primary sources, CSV/downloads and signed-in screen content before claiming full audit\"],\"publication_block\":\"Cloudflare error 10000; no writes to production\"}")
draft=json.loads("{\"as_of\":\"2026-10-03\",\"status\":\"prepared_not_published\",\"production_writes\":0,\"clinical_certification\":false,\"independent_clinical_review_required\":false,\"publication_block\":\"Cloudflare authentication error 10000\",\"scope\":\"Public editorial content only; AI systems excluded\",\"publication_requirements\":[\"Fresh public-article database snapshot and current production baseline\",\"Preserve original publication date, canonical URL and authorship; update modification date only on publish\",\"Replace full article body and corresponding metadata/structured data consistently\",\"Compare-and-swap guards against concurrent edits\",\"Review rendered page and public source links before production sign-off\"],\"articles\":[{\"path\":\"/articles/evidence-based-weight-loss\",\"title\":\"Evidence-based weight management: a plan that fits your health\",\"reason\":\"Remove unsupported commercial endorsements, guaranteed timelines, biological blame and false claims that SHIFT currently provides clinical treatment.\",\"body\":\"Weight management can involve food, movement, behavioural support, medicines or surgery. The useful question is which combination fits your health, circumstances and preferences. A trial average cannot predict your result.\\n\\n## Start with a useful assessment\\nTell your healthcare professional what you have already tried, what was difficult and what you want to improve. Include medicines, sleep, mobility, eating difficulties and relevant health conditions. Weight and BMI can inform assessment, but they do not describe your whole health or determine treatment on their own.\\n\\n## Build changes you can sustain\\nChoose practical food and movement changes that fit your budget, ability and ordinary week. A fixed calorie deficit does not guarantee a particular weekly loss. Very restrictive diets need appropriate assessment and support; they are not a default starting point. [NICE’s guidance explains the place of dietary approaches](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng246/chapter/Physical-activity-and-diet).\\n\\nStart activity at a manageable level and build gradually. Walking, adapted activity and strength work can all have a place. There is no requirement to achieve a full weekly activity target before asking for treatment.\\n\\n## Understand what treatment evidence means\\nA medicine’s authorisation, an NHS funding recommendation and your individual suitability are separate decisions. Products have different instructions, eligibility rules and review points. Ask the prescriber which evidence applies to the exact medicine and formulation being discussed. [NICE sets out medicine-specific recommendations](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng246/chapter/Medicines-and-surgery).\\n\\nWhen comparing studies, check the participants, duration, dose, comparison group, withdrawals and how missing results were handled. Do not rank treatments by percentages from unrelated trials.\\n\\n## Plan follow-up and maintenance\\nAgree how benefit, side effects, nutrition and daily functioning will be reviewed. If treatment changes or ends, ask what support remains available. Regain can occur after weight-loss medicine is stopped; it is not proof of failed effort. Habits matter, but they do not remove the biological effects of stopping treatment.\\n\\nSHIFT provides information and everyday planning tools. Its proposed partner treatment service is not open for booking. Reading this article does not arrange a consultation, prescription or clinical follow-up.\\n\\n## One useful next step\\nWrite down the change that matters most to you, one barrier you face and the questions you want to discuss. Take that to your GP or existing care team.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/glp-1-and-exercise\",\"title\":\"Exercise while taking weight-loss medicine: start from your level\",\"reason\":\"Replace compulsory exercise, universal protein targets, unsupported product recommendations and guaranteed muscle protection with adaptable guidance.\",\"body\":\"Movement can support health while you take weight-loss medicine, but your starting point, symptoms and medical history matter. A general activity guideline is a direction to work towards, not a test you must pass.\\n\\n## Build a manageable week\\nThe NHS advises adults to aim for at least 150 minutes of moderate activity or 75 minutes of vigorous activity each week, plus strengthening activity on two days. Spread activity through the week, reduce long periods of sitting and build gradually. [Read the NHS adult activity guidance](https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/).\\n\\nFor a beginner, a short walk or suitable seated session may be a useful start. Sixty minutes in a week is progress from none; it should not be labelled as meeting a 150-minute target. Age, disability, illness and recovery can call for adapted advice.\\n\\n## Include strength where appropriate\\nStrength activity can include supported body-weight movements or resistance equipment. Choose a level you can manage safely. If pain, an injury or a health condition limits you, ask a physiotherapist or healthcare professional about suitable options. No routine can guarantee that you will lose no muscle during weight loss.\\n\\n## Eating and recovery still matter\\nReduced appetite can make it harder to eat enough varied food. Include protein-containing foods in meals, but do not assume one gram target or supplement suits everyone. Kidney disease and other conditions can change dietary advice. [Guy’s and St Thomas’ nutrition guidance](https://www.guysandstthomas.nhs.uk/health-information/lifestyle-advice-people-taking-weight-loss-medicines/food-and-nutrition) is a useful starting point; a dietitian can help with individual needs.\\n\\nFeeling unwell is a reason to reassess, not push through a prescribed session. Persistent vomiting, difficulty drinking, weakness or worsening symptoms need medical advice. Your prescriber should review troublesome side effects.\\n\\n## Know when to get help\\nStop exercise if you develop concerning symptoms. Severe chest pain, collapse or severe breathing difficulty needs emergency help. Severe, persistent abdominal pain, sometimes spreading to the back, needs urgent medical attention because it can indicate pancreatitis. [See the MHRA warning](https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/glp-1-receptor-agonists-and-dual-glp-1-slash-gip-receptor-agonists-strengthened-warnings-on-acute-pancreatitis-including-necrotising-and-fatal-cases).\\n\\nSHIFT’s movement tools support planning; they do not assess medical fitness or replace your treatment team. Choose one suitable activity you can repeat, and review how you feel.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/glp-1-coaching\",\"title\":\"Coaching alongside weight-loss medicine: what useful support looks like\",\"reason\":\"Remove unsupported service specifications and confused clinical roles; distinguish coaching from prescribing.\",\"body\":\"Coaching can help you plan meals, movement and routines around everyday life. It does not decide whether a medicine is suitable, diagnose symptoms or authorise dose changes.\\n\\n## Know who does what\\nYour prescriber is responsible for treatment decisions and clinical review. A coach may help you work towards agreed goals, while a registered dietitian can provide individual nutrition advice within their professional role. Ask about qualifications, boundaries and how concerns reach your clinical team.\\n\\nA useful support conversation starts with your circumstances. It can cover shopping, appetite changes, work patterns, mobility and setbacks without turning a slow week into a failure.\\n\\n## Ask before joining\\nFind out what is included, how often contact happens, whether the service is individual or group-based, and what it costs. Ask who responds when you are unwell, what happens outside opening hours and whether support continues if treatment stops. Do not assume a coaching subscription includes prescribing or emergency care.\\n\\nNHS England describes behavioural support around primary-care tirzepatide through its wraparound-care programme. This is a specific NHS arrangement, not a universal specification for every private or NHS service. [Read the NHS England explanation](https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/tirzepatide-in-primary-care-for-weight-management-information-on-wraparound-care/).\\n\\n## Bring treatment questions to the prescriber\\nReport persistent side effects and problems eating or drinking. Follow the instructions for your exact medicine; do not change dose or timing on a coach’s advice alone. Pregnancy planning, breastfeeding, other medicines and upcoming procedures also belong in the clinical conversation. [The MHRA patient guide explains key precautions](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/glp-1-medicines-for-weight-loss-and-diabetes-what-you-need-to-know/glp-1-medicines-for-weight-loss-and-diabetes-what-you-need-to-know).\\n\\n## What SHIFT offers today\\nSHIFT’s information and member tools can help you keep goals and everyday progress together. The proposed partner prescribing service is not open for booking. A check-in does not create clinical monitoring or a prescription.\\n\\nUseful support makes the next step clearer. It should also be honest about what it cannot provide.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/glp-1-for-men\",\"title\":\"Weight-loss medicines for men: questions worth asking\",\"reason\":\"Remove blanket NHS eligibility, unproven hormone/fertility benefits, mood promises and class-wide titration instructions.\",\"body\":\"Being a man does not create a separate shortcut to a weight-loss prescription. Suitability depends on the exact product, your health, other medicines and an individual assessment.\\n\\n## Separate eligibility from availability\\nA medicine’s licensed indication is not the same as the criteria for NHS funding. NHS routes also differ between UK nations and local services. Do not apply one BMI threshold to every medicine or assume a calculator confirms access. [NICE’s medicine guidance sets out distinct recommendations](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng246/chapter/Medicines-and-surgery).\\n\\nTell the clinician about previous treatment, side effects, diabetes medicines, digestive symptoms and any relevant medical history. Include medicines obtained privately or online.\\n\\n## Keep wider health in the conversation\\nEnergy, sleep, sexual function and mood deserve attention in their own right. Weight-loss medicine should not be presented as a treatment for low testosterone, infertility or erection difficulties without a separate assessment and relevant indication. Persistent symptoms warrant a GP conversation rather than a promise that weight loss will resolve them.\\n\\nIf you already take blood-pressure or diabetes treatment, ask how it will be reviewed as your health changes. Do not reduce medicines yourself because your weight has fallen.\\n\\n## Know your exact instructions\\nProducts differ in formulation, dose escalation and missed-dose advice. Follow your own prescription and patient leaflet rather than another person’s schedule. Ask who you can contact if side effects interfere with eating, drinking or daily life.\\n\\nSevere, persistent abdominal pain, including pain spreading to the back, needs urgent medical attention. Do not dismiss it as routine nausea. [The MHRA explains the pancreatitis warning](https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/glp-1-receptor-agonists-and-dual-glp-1-slash-gip-receptor-agonists-strengthened-warnings-on-acute-pancreatitis-including-necrotising-and-fatal-cases).\\n\\n## Agree what success means\\nDiscuss weight alongside the health and daily-life outcomes that matter to you. Ask when treatment will be reviewed, what would prompt a change and how support would continue if you stopped.\\n\\nSHIFT can help you explore information and organise everyday goals. Partner treatment is not currently bookable through SHIFT.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/nhs-weight-loss-drugs\",\"title\":\"NHS weight-loss medicines: understand the route before the threshold\",\"reason\":\"Correct conflation of product-specific NHS criteria, England versus UK rules, private regulation and treatment duration.\",\"body\":\"There is no single NHS eligibility rule covering every weight-loss medicine. The product, your health, the UK nation you live in and the local service all matter.\\n\\n## Three different decisions\\nA UK medicines licence defines authorised uses. NICE recommendations assess particular uses for NHS care. A clinician then decides whether treatment is appropriate for you within the service available. A licence or a BMI result alone does not guarantee an NHS prescription.\\n\\nMedicines used in weight management include options with different mechanisms, instructions and funding criteria. A short list of injection brands is not a complete description of NHS treatment.\\n\\n## Tirzepatide access in England\\nNHS England’s phased implementation distinguishes specialist weight-management services from primary-care access. Initial primary-care cohorts are narrower than the full NICE recommendation. Local arrangements and the current phase therefore matter. [Check NHS England’s commissioning guidance](https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/interim-commissioning-guidance-nice-ta1026-tirzepatide/) and your local NHS service.\\n\\nEngland’s integrated care board arrangements should not be described as rules for the whole UK. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have their own routes and implementation decisions.\\n\\n## Other medicines have their own rules\\nSemaglutide, liraglutide, tirzepatide and orlistat should not be given an interchangeable eligibility or stopping rule. A recommendation for a particular formulation does not automatically establish funding for every formulation of the same ingredient. [Use NICE’s medicine-by-medicine guidance](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng246/chapter/Medicines-and-surgery), then confirm what your local service provides.\\n\\n## Private care still needs proper safeguards\\nPrivate prescribing is not outside regulation. Registered professionals and pharmacies have professional responsibilities, including when services operate online. Equally, a polished website does not establish that a supplier is legitimate. Ask who prescribes, who dispenses, how suitability is checked and who handles follow-up. [The GPhC’s review describes its oversight of weight-management services](https://assets.pharmacyregulation.org/files/2026-04/Weight-management-medicines-and-services-a-review-of-GPhC-inspections-and-concerns-April-2026.pdf).\\n\\nPaying privately does not guarantee a later transfer to NHS prescribing. Discuss the whole cost and continuity plan before starting.\\n\\n## Your next step\\nAsk your GP or local service which pathway is open, whether you meet its current criteria and what support is available while you wait. SHIFT’s proposed partner treatment service is not open for booking and cannot confirm NHS eligibility.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/semaglutide-results-timeline\",\"title\":\"Semaglutide results: why there is no personal countdown\",\"reason\":\"Remove unsupported day-by-day promises, formulation conflation and the incorrect six-month maintenance-dose checkpoint.\",\"body\":\"Semaglutide studies report results over months. They do not establish a day when every person should notice appetite change or a week when weight loss must accelerate.\\n\\n## Start with the exact product\\nSemaglutide is available in different products and formulations with different indications and instructions. Do not transfer an injection schedule to a tablet or assume every semaglutide product is licensed for weight management. Your prescription and patient leaflet are the starting point.\\n\\nDose increases are treatment decisions, not milestones to rush towards. Tell the prescriber about side effects or difficulty eating and drinking before a planned increase.\\n\\n## Follow a trend, not one weigh-in\\nIf weighing is useful for you, use a consistent method and review a series of measurements. Short-term changes do not establish whether treatment is succeeding or failing. Discuss health, nutrition, symptoms and everyday functioning alongside weight.\\n\\nTrial averages describe groups with defined entry criteria, doses, support and follow-up. They are not a promised personal result. Comparisons need the same attention to trial design and duration.\\n\\n## Know which review point applies\\nFor semaglutide in NICE’s weight-management recommendation, consider stopping if less than 5% of initial weight has been lost after six months of treatment. This is a clinical review decision, not an instruction to stop on your own. It is not six months after reaching a maintenance dose. [Read NICE TA875](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta875/chapter/1-Recommendations).\\n\\nThat recommendation should not be used as a blanket description of every semaglutide indication, formulation or private treatment plan. Ask your prescriber which review framework applies to you.\\n\\n## Do not wait out concerning symptoms\\nThere is no fixed deadline by which side effects must settle. Seek advice if symptoms persist or interfere with drinking, eating or daily life. Sudden loss of vision needs urgent assessment. [The NHS semaglutide guide covers medicine use and safety](https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/semaglutide/).\\n\\nA plateau is a reason for a conversation, not proof that you need an unplanned higher dose. Bring your treatment history, actual doses and concerns to the review.\\n\\nSHIFT’s tools can help you organise observations. They do not change your prescription or provide clinical monitoring.\"},{\"path\":\"/articles/wegovy-not-working\",\"title\":\"Wegovy not working as expected? Prepare for a useful review\",\"reason\":\"Remove blame, mandatory waiting periods, blanket continuation advice and unsupported diagnostic testing.\",\"body\":\"A slower result than expected deserves a proper review. It does not establish that you have failed, that you are eating dishonestly or that the medicine cannot help.\\n\\n## Check what you are comparing\\nSocial-media results and trial averages are not personal targets. Your product, dose, time on treatment, health and side effects affect how a clinician interprets progress.\\n\\nKeep a brief record of actual doses, missed doses, weight trends if useful, symptoms and any difficulty eating or drinking. Bring the exact product details to your review. If storage or administration is uncertain, ask the dispensing pharmacist; do not take an extra dose to make up for uncertainty.\\n\\n## Review the whole picture\\nA prescriber can discuss response, tolerability, other medicines and whether further assessment would help. Do not order a universal hormone or blood-test panel because an article says everyone needs it. HbA1c assesses longer-term blood glucose; it is not a direct diagnostic test for insulin resistance.\\n\\nThere is no rule that everyone must wait a fixed number of weeks before raising a concern. Problems with side effects, nutrition or treatment instructions can need earlier advice.\\n\\n## Understand the formal checkpoint\\nNICE’s semaglutide weight-management recommendation says to consider stopping if weight loss is below 5% of the starting weight after six months of treatment. It does not say six months at maintenance dose. Discuss the recommendation with the prescriber rather than treating it as a self-directed dosing rule. [Read NICE TA875](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta875/chapter/1-Recommendations).\\n\\nDose escalation, a different treatment or stopping all require an individual decision. A higher dose is not automatically the right answer.\\n\\n## Safety comes before the timetable\\nSevere and continuing abdominal pain, including pain reaching the back, needs urgent medical attention. [Read the MHRA pancreatitis warning](https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/glp-1-receptor-agonists-and-dual-glp-1-slash-gip-receptor-agonists-strengthened-warnings-on-acute-pancreatitis-including-necrotising-and-fatal-cases). Do not keep taking treatment just to reach a review date when urgent assessment is needed.\\n\\n## Questions for the appointment\\nAsk what benefit has been seen so far, whether the current plan remains appropriate, how side effects will be handled and what support would continue if treatment changed.\\n\\nSHIFT can help you organise these questions. It does not prescribe Wegovy, and its proposed partner treatment route is not open for booking.\"}]}")
